      "dcterms:format": "application/json",
      "dcterms:description": "{\"action\":\"\",\"actor\":\"\",\"alternative_quotes\":[],\"applies_to\":[],\"assertion_ids\":[\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:f96a6882b82158944e4fa8d40acc46a732c7264316f231d8bd1e1066f10e2915\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:0d0b1dd5e11c7122a153cd076a8485d4bbfe593410e0f05799ff944e9d376be1\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:160108583f6ca9302c3090eff3b11adabae3b133194f74f3e3f79f35b6cb3160\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:38ae3dd9ec6e216f5b8387b8e645295a4211632d77b49158a6a82779b0e8770d\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:2dba7677fa4ffec936c98e5c427ad9fe74608e116b43831e0cb83b0cc74eb286\"],\"choice_quote\":\"\",\"choice_text\":\"\",\"claimants\":[],\"concepts\":[],\"context_quotes\":[\"(1) Subject to the exceptions provided in paragraph (b)(2) of this section, employment periods prior to a break in service of seven years or more need not be counted in determining whether the employee has been employed by the employer for at least 12 months.\\n\\n(2) Employment periods preceding a break in service of more than seven years must be counted in determining whether the employee has been employed by the employer for at least 12 months where:\\n\\n(i) The employee's break in service is occasioned by the fulfillment of his or her Uniformed Services Employment and Reemployment Rights Act (USERRA), 38 U.S.C. 4301, et seq., covered service obligation. The period of absence from work due to or necessitated by USERRA-covered service must be also counted in determining whether the employee has been employed for at least 12 months by the employer. However, this section does not provide any greater entitlement to the employee than would be available under the USERRA; or\\n\\n(ii) A written agreement, including a collective bargaining agreement, exists concerning the employer's intention to rehire the employee after the break in service (e.g., for purposes of the employee furthering his or her education or for childrearing purposes).\"],\"effective_periods\":[],\"end\":2804,\"evidence\":[{\"end\":2804,\"field\":\"summary\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:2419:2804:sha256-668999c51c74d480383126ed520172cea18a50cba1239cdc7337af5ed0e7a425\",\"quote\":\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\",\"start\":2419},{\"end\":2804,\"field\":\"logic_text\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:2419:2804:sha256-668999c51c74d480383126ed520172cea18a50cba1239cdc7337af5ed0e7a425\",\"quote\":\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\",\"start\":2419},{\"end\":2455,\"field\":\"modality\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:2447:2455:sha256-4280084b50f9ffe730922d99519e1b2e7c3eab1209766993d9cfdd3759cdee01\",\"quote\":\"prevents\",\"start\":2447},{\"end\":2804,\"field\":\"scope_text:0\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:2419:2804:sha256-668999c51c74d480383126ed520172cea18a50cba1239cdc7337af5ed0e7a425\",\"quote\":\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\",\"start\":2419},{\"end\":1946,\"field\":\"context:0\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:704:1946:sha256-b1d9796e677a08e4482d718189c5f4da5471bdb60c5eeb17662168cd3efebbd2\",\"quote\":\"(1) Subject to the exceptions provided in paragraph (b)(2) of this section, employment periods prior to a break in service of seven years or more need not be counted in determining whether the employee has been employed by the employer for at least 12 months.\\n\\n(2) Employment periods preceding a break in service of more than seven years must be counted in determining whether the employee has been employed by the employer for at least 12 months where:\\n\\n(i) The employee's break in service is occasioned by the fulfillment of his or her Uniformed Services Employment and Reemployment Rights Act (USERRA), 38 U.S.C. 4301, et seq., covered service obligation. The period of absence from work due to or necessitated by USERRA-covered service must be also counted in determining whether the employee has been employed for at least 12 months by the employer. However, this section does not provide any greater entitlement to the employee than would be available under the USERRA; or\\n\\n(ii) A written agreement, including a collective bargaining agreement, exists concerning the employer's intention to rehire the employee after the break in service (e.g., for purposes of the employee furthering his or her education or for childrearing purposes).\",\"start\":704}],\"id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:revision:7d0274e04b04f983824f8efb4ddc37e11ed7a96d3aa1df05d510d33e8a4a9c0a\",\"jurisdiction\":\"\",\"jurisdiction_quote\":\"\",\"kind\":\"permission\",\"logic_text\":\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\",\"meaning_assertion_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:2dba7677fa4ffec936c98e5c427ad9fe74608e116b43831e0cb83b0cc74eb286\",\"modality\":\"may\",\"modality_quote\":\"prevents\",\"object\":\"\",\"occurrence_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:occurrence:d9763a6e2eaa2d8a771ce4b059065c9ec0877e27c4fbb4a5e21d19e51917c104\",\"origin\":\"aiSuggested\",\"quote\":\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\",\"references\":[],\"relation\":\"none\",\"rule_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:rule:c56f7b8a6c17b7f84b3931e795fc3b0f213ff518e749ee45525f466e42dc93e9\",\"scope_quotes\":[\"(4) Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement. However, if an employer chooses to recognize such prior employment, the employer must do so uniformly, with respect to all employees with similar breaks in service.\"],\"scope_text\":\"When determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement\",\"section_id\":\"section-1\",\"start\":2419,\"summary\":\"Nothing in this section prevents employers from considering employment prior to a continuous break in service of more than seven years when determining whether an employee has met the 12-month employment requirement.\",\"target_ids\":[],\"typed_values\":[]}",
