    {
      "@id": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:revision:7e009a74d86e1d47a431e5baedffd0331289d35030ffcc2f8424892bddf2e442",
      "@type": "rkaf:Artifact",
      "rkaf:hasArtifactIdentifier": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:revision:7e009a74d86e1d47a431e5baedffd0331289d35030ffcc2f8424892bddf2e442",
      "rkaf:artifactIdentifierScheme": "rkaf:urn-persistent",
      "rkaf:hasContentDigest": "sha256:59bf69359032e491a0220bc248ea46d299e869357b03e9294c01a10d5c0624bc",
      "dcterms:format": "application/json",
      "dcterms:description": "{\"action\":\"\",\"actor\":\"\",\"alternative_quotes\":[],\"applies_to\":[],\"assertion_ids\":[\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:88fae2db1d7f2e2a7bab49648db4fe35449358d6c8a9b1a7ff04eec4f6986165\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:67ae60b000a37a11e28e6351120a7d1a6f85c916fb8a290c3e698d07925bd4ce\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:0fb33b9b4b9ed4b42c43c7e9f432e6ea914ffab31128197e13b443d0f319fd52\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:6a269e1b6a77b6b9f0c5662b454ce642a34acc3ccfe83ed6fb22c3cd4d2a2ed0\",\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:8368a3f33c1ea6762ca91de76529765babc768717e5fcfe52bde8bb6e9d956ef\"],\"choice_quote\":\"\",\"choice_text\":\"\",\"claimants\":[],\"concepts\":[],\"context_quotes\":[\"(c)(1) Except as provided in paragraph (c)(2) of this section and in § 825.801 containing the special hours of service requirement for airline flight crew employees, whether an employee has worked the minimum 1,250 hours of service is determined according to the principles established under the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) for determining compensable hours of work. See 29 CFR part 785. The determining factor is the number of hours an employee has worked for the employer within the meaning of the FLSA. The determination is not limited by methods of recordkeeping, or by compensation agreements that do not accurately reflect all of the hours an employee has worked for or been in service to the employer. Any accurate accounting of actual hours worked under FLSA's principles may be used.\"],\"effective_periods\":[],\"end\":5368,\"evidence\":[{\"end\":5368,\"field\":\"summary\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:4440:5368:sha256-f224bb89288a47851c4a1b3b1e2aceb37d95d77172afee44175f2f36b851f8ae\",\"quote\":\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\",\"start\":4440},{\"end\":5368,\"field\":\"logic_text\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:4440:5368:sha256-f224bb89288a47851c4a1b3b1e2aceb37d95d77172afee44175f2f36b851f8ae\",\"quote\":\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\",\"start\":4440},{\"end\":4793,\"field\":\"modality\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:4779:4793:sha256-ecf616a51ed28ce33bbfedadf935f65406371a28a340ee76dac503b7ea81ec06\",\"quote\":\"has the burden\",\"start\":4779},{\"end\":5368,\"field\":\"scope_text:0\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:4440:5368:sha256-f224bb89288a47851c4a1b3b1e2aceb37d95d77172afee44175f2f36b851f8ae\",\"quote\":\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\",\"start\":4440},{\"end\":3602,\"field\":\"context:0\",\"fragment_id\":\"urn:rkaf:fragment:urn%3Arulespec%3Adocument-understanding%3Adocument%3Aa4cdfc9b75c8a1ed78c29b8a344913757e4b95198a4b069127aa9092960aaf4f:2806:3602:sha256-0207e56b05e1baa2b73068ae0df66fa509d96f5833d29bab4fe4be2d62b8fd28\",\"quote\":\"(c)(1) Except as provided in paragraph (c)(2) of this section and in § 825.801 containing the special hours of service requirement for airline flight crew employees, whether an employee has worked the minimum 1,250 hours of service is determined according to the principles established under the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) for determining compensable hours of work. See 29 CFR part 785. The determining factor is the number of hours an employee has worked for the employer within the meaning of the FLSA. The determination is not limited by methods of recordkeeping, or by compensation agreements that do not accurately reflect all of the hours an employee has worked for or been in service to the employer. Any accurate accounting of actual hours worked under FLSA's principles may be used.\",\"start\":2806}],\"id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:revision:7e009a74d86e1d47a431e5baedffd0331289d35030ffcc2f8424892bddf2e442\",\"jurisdiction\":\"\",\"jurisdiction_quote\":\"\",\"kind\":\"requirement\",\"logic_text\":\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\",\"meaning_assertion_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:8368a3f33c1ea6762ca91de76529765babc768717e5fcfe52bde8bb6e9d956ef\",\"modality\":\"must\",\"modality_quote\":\"has the burden\",\"object\":\"\",\"occurrence_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:occurrence:54f8d5443aaf8fa20c497b75dc36217c2b26a9d64c4de322a61898370baf64e1\",\"origin\":\"aiSuggested\",\"quote\":\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\",\"references\":[\"29 CFR part 541\",\"FLSA\",\"§ 825.102\",\"§ 825.801(d)\"],\"relation\":\"none\",\"rule_id\":\"urn:rulespec:document-understanding:rule:ad4aef7a3eb060a574fd6717847d35e73a0d0618b495dd08ee590fec2fd87f2f\",\"scope_quotes\":[\"(3) In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's requirement that a record be kept of their hours worked (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees as defined in FLSA Regulations, 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours. An employer must be able to clearly demonstrate, for example, that full-time teachers ( see § 825.102 for definition) of an elementary or secondary school system, or institution of higher education, or other educational establishment or institution (who often work outside the classroom or at their homes) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim that the teachers are not eligible for FMLA leave. See § 825.801(d) for special rules applicable to airline flight crew employees.\"],\"scope_text\":\"In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee, including for employees who are exempt from FLSA's recordkeeping requirement (e.g., bona fide executive, administrative, and professional employees under 29 CFR part 541)\",\"section_id\":\"section-1\",\"start\":4440,\"summary\":\"In the event an employer does not maintain an accurate record of hours worked by an employee (including employees exempt from FLSA recordkeeping under 29 CFR part 541), the employer has the burden of showing that the employee has not worked the requisite hours, and must be able to clearly demonstrate that the employee (such as full-time teachers of an elementary, secondary, or higher education establishment) did not work 1,250 hours during the previous 12 months in order to claim ineligibility for FMLA leave (subject to § 825.801(d) for airline flight crew employees).\",\"target_ids\":[],\"typed_values\":[]}",
      "dcterms:references": [
        {
          "@id": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:88fae2db1d7f2e2a7bab49648db4fe35449358d6c8a9b1a7ff04eec4f6986165"
        },
        {
          "@id": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:assertion:67ae60b000a37a11e28e6351120a7d1a6f85c916fb8a290c3e698d07925bd4ce"
        },
        {
